dict(
slot="Diaspora", label="DIASPORA VOICE", cats=[8, 12],
slug="ghana-protests-iraq-assault-domestic-worker-recruitment-moratorium",
title="Ghana Protests to Iraq Over Assault on Domestic Worker and Weighs a Halt to Recruitment",
excerpt="Foreign Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa says Ghana has formally protested to Iraq after a viral video showed Stephanie Nkrumah being assaulted, and is considering a moratorium on recruiting domestic workers there.",
kw="Ghana Iraq domestic worker, Stephanie Nkrumah, Ablakwa moratorium, African migrant workers Gulf, labour migration Ghana",
seo_title="Ghana Protests to Iraq, Weighs Domestic Worker Ban",
desc="Ghana has protested to Iraq over the assault of domestic worker Stephanie Nkrumah and is weighing a recruitment moratorium. What it means for migrant workers.",
tags=["Ghana","Iraq","Stephanie Nkrumah","Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa","Domestic Workers","Labour Migration","African Diaspora","Diaspora Voice","Migrant Rights","Middle East","Human Trafficking","Ghana Foreign Affairs"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>ACCRA</strong> — Ghana has formally protested to Iraqi authorities over the assault of a Ghanaian domestic worker and is considering a moratorium on recruiting domestic workers to Iraq, Foreign Affairs Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa said this week.</p>

<p>The worker, Stephanie Nkrumah, appeared in a viral video showing what Ablakwa called a "violent and dehumanising assault" by her employer. She escaped and returned to Ghana with help from her family, <a href="https://www.citinewsroom.com/2026/11/ghana-considers-iraq-recruitment-ban-after-domestic-worker-assault/" {A}>Citi Newsroom reported</a>, quoting the minister's Facebook post of Tuesday, 24 November.</p>

<h2>What the government has said</h2>

<p>Ablakwa said he met Nkrumah and her family on Monday, 23 November, after officials worked to identify her from the video. The family lives in Kumawu in the Ashanti Region, according to the <a href="https://allafrica.com/stories/202611250295.html" {A}>Ghanaian Times</a>.</p>

<p>"We listened carefully to her narrate her ordeal and encouraged her to share with us details of her abusers and their network to assist us in our diplomatic efforts for justice," he wrote.</p>

<p>The minister said the government had communicated its displeasure to Iraq and was demanding "appropriate action" against those responsible. He added that the case did not appear to be isolated, which is why a recruitment moratorium is under consideration.</p>

<p>The government is paying for urgent medical care and clinical psychological support for Nkrumah, the Ghanaian Times reported. Ablakwa also personally gave her GH¢50,000 towards a small business and pledged a scholarship so she can return to formal education.</p>

<p>"An attack on any Ghanaian out there, is an attack on all of us," he said, as quoted by <a href="https://newsghana.com.gh/ghana-weighs-iraq-domestic-workers-ban-after-alleged-assault/" {A}>News Ghana</a>.</p>

<h2>No ban yet, and no arrests announced</h2>

<p>No moratorium is in force and no date has been given for a decision, News Ghana noted. It also said no arrest or prosecution had been announced and that it had found no response from Iraqi authorities or the employer. Fintech Telex has also not seen a public response from Iraq.</p>

<p>Ghana has acted before. News Ghana pointed to a temporary ban on recruiting workers to Gulf countries in 2017 after a rise in reported abuse, and a Ghana–Qatar labour mobility agreement ratified by Parliament in December 2025. A 2023 International Organization for Migration report counted nearly 200 recruitment agencies operating in Ghana, News Ghana added.</p>

<h2>What it means for Africans working abroad</h2>

<p>Domestic work in the Middle East remains a common route abroad for young Africans looking for income to send home. Cases like this one show the risks when recruitment agents, employers and visa sponsors operate with little oversight.</p>

<p>A moratorium would protect workers in the short term but could push some people towards unlicensed agents. That is why labour agreements, licensed recruiters and embassies that can respond quickly matter as much as bans.</p>

<p>For families, the practical advice is the same across West Africa: check that any recruiter is licensed, keep copies of contracts and passports, register with the nearest embassy on arrival, and save emergency contacts before travel.</p>

<p>For governments, the test is follow-through. Ablakwa has promised continued support for Nkrumah's pursuit of justice. Whether Iraq acts on Ghana's protest, and whether Accra pairs any moratorium with safer legal routes, will decide how much changes for the next worker.</p>

<p>Fintech Telex will update this story if Iraqi authorities respond or Ghana announces a decision on recruitment.</p>
"""),
