dict(
slot="Security", label="SECURITY NEWS", cats=[17],
slug="sa-home-loans-data-breach-bank-details-debit-order-fraud-warning",
title="SA Home Loans Hit by Second Data Breach This Year as Customers' Bank Account Details May Be Exposed",
excerpt="South Africa's SA Home Loans says an unknown attacker accessed systems including its collections platform on 13 November, possibly exposing names, account numbers and branch codes, and warns of debit order fraud.",
kw="SA Home Loans data breach, debit order fraud, South Africa cybersecurity, RelyComply breach, protect bank details",
seo_title="SA Home Loans Data Breach May Expose Bank Details",
desc="SA Home Loans says an attacker accessed its collections system, possibly exposing bank account details. What customers should do and what it means for lenders.",
tags=["SA Home Loans","Data Breach","South Africa","Debit Order Fraud","Cybersecurity","POPIA","Information Regulator","RelyComply","Phishing","Security News","Financial Services","SAFPS"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>JOHANNESBURG</strong> — South African mortgage provider SA Home Loans (SAHL) has warned customers that an unknown attacker gained access to some of its systems, including one used to collect payments, and may have obtained their bank account details.</p>

<p>"On 13 November 2026, we detected unauthorised access by an unknown third party to certain of our systems, including a system used for collections," the company said in a notice quoted by <a href="https://mybroadband.co.za/news/security/679913-south-african-home-loans-giant-hit-by-second-data-breach-in-3-months-with-customer-bank-account-details-exposed.html" {A}>MyBroadband</a>.</p>

<h2>What may have been exposed</h2>

<p>The information accessed may include customers' names, bank account numbers and branch codes, "and some details of payments made by us", SAHL said, according to <a href="https://techcentral.co.za/sa-home-loans-data-breach/288689/" {A}>TechCentral</a>.</p>

<p>SAHL said there was no evidence that home loan accounts were accessed or that customer records were altered, and no financial loss had been identified. "We do not know the identity of the threat actor at this stage," it said.</p>

<p>The company said it contained the incident, removed the attacker's access, hired independent forensic specialists and increased security monitoring. It notified the Information Regulator, as required under the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA), along with other regulators and law enforcement.</p>

<h2>The second incident in months</h2>

<p>This is the second breach affecting SAHL customers this year. In September, a breach at compliance firm RelyComply, a service provider to SAHL, exposed customer data including names and ID or passport numbers, MyBroadband reported. An extortion group called Dire Wolf claimed responsibility for that attack, which also affected customers of other South African financial firms.</p>

<p><a href="https://techcabal.com/2026/11/26/%F0%9F%91%A8%F0%9F%8F%BF%F0%9F%9A%80techcabal-daily-a-house-breach/" {A}>TechCabal</a> noted that the September incident came through a service provider, while the November breach involved SAHL's own systems.</p>

<p>The scale of the sector shows what is at stake. South Africa had about 1.62 million mortgage accounts worth R1.31 trillion as of June 2026, according to National Credit Regulator figures cited by TechCabal. <a href="https://www.itweb.co.za/article/sa-home-loans-hit-by-second-data-breach/j5alrvQAXBzvpYQk" {A}>ITWeb</a> also reported on the latest incident.</p>

<h2>The risk: fake debit orders and phishing</h2>

<p>A name, account number and branch code are enough to attempt a fraudulent debit order, TechCentral noted. SAHL warned that criminals may also use the information to send phishing emails and SMS messages, or call customers pretending to be from SAHL or their bank, and that the data could be published online.</p>

<p>The company advised customers to:</p>
<ul>
<li>check bank statements and dispute any unfamiliar debit orders;</li>
<li>never share PINs, one-time PINs or passwords with anyone;</li>
<li>be cautious of links and unexpected requests for information;</li>
<li>consider a free credit report or Protective Verification through the Southern African Fraud Prevention Service.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What it means for African lenders and customers</h2>

<p>Lenders hold exactly the data fraudsters want: verified identities, bank details and payment schedules. As more African banks, fintechs and mortgage providers outsource identity checks and collections, each supplier becomes another possible point of failure.</p>

<p>For companies, the lesson is to map every system and partner that touches customer banking data, limit what each one stores, and rehearse breach notification. For customers in South Africa, Nigeria, Kenya and elsewhere, the rule is simple: your lender or bank will never ask for your OTP. Treat any such request as a scam.</p>
"""),
