dict(
slot="AI", label="ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE", cats=[2, 642],
slug="inec-ai-unit-deepfakes-2027-elections-yiaga-transparency",
title="INEC Says Its AI Unit Will Counter Deepfakes Before Nigeria's 2027 Elections as Experts Call for Transparency",
excerpt="INEC chairman Joash Amupitan says the commission's AI division will help detect and respond to deepfakes and cloned voices, while Yiaga Africa says the commission has not explained its AI approach clearly.",
kw="INEC AI unit, deepfakes Nigeria election, AI disinformation 2027, Yiaga Africa AI, election technology Africa",
seo_title="INEC's AI Unit to Fight Deepfakes Before 2027 Elections",
desc="INEC says its AI division will help counter deepfakes and cloned voices before Nigeria's 2027 vote, as Yiaga Africa urges transparency. What voters should know.",
tags=["INEC","Artificial Intelligence","Deepfakes","Disinformation","Nigeria 2027 Elections","Yiaga Africa","CJID","Fact-Checking","Election Technology","AI Policy","BVAS","Nigeria"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>ABUJA</strong> — Nigeria's Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) says its artificial intelligence (AI) division will help it detect and respond to deepfakes, cloned voices and coordinated false narratives before the 2027 general elections.</p>

<p>INEC chairman Joash Amupitan made the remarks in a keynote address at the Nigeria Election Risk and Readiness Summit 2026 in Abuja on Wednesday, delivered on his behalf by his chief press secretary, Adedayo Oketola, <a href="https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/11/inec-deploys-ai-unit-to-combat-misinformation-ahead-of-2027-polls/" {A}>Vanguard reported</a>. The summit was convened by the Centre for Journalism Innovation and Development (CJID).</p>

<h2>What INEC says the threat is</h2>

<p>The commission said generative AI, synthetic voice cloning, deepfakes and coordinated online campaigns have made information management a critical part of election security. Fabricated audio, doctored videos and false results could trigger panic, discourage turnout and spark communal violence, Amupitan said.</p>

<p>He cited a viral video during the Federal Capital Territory election that falsely claimed National Youth Service Corps members were being killed in Abuja on polling day. INEC said it worked with the NYSC to verify and debunk the claim.</p>

<p>INEC called for institutional partnerships with fact-checkers such as Dubawa, media organisations, civil society groups and the National Orientation Agency, and pledged timely access to official information for verified journalists.</p>

<h2>Not a new unit, and open questions</h2>

<p>The AI division sits within INEC's ICT department and was first approved in May 2025, <a href="https://www.riotimesonline.com/inec-ai-unit-deepfakes-2027-election-nigeria/" {A}>The Rio Times</a> reported. In October, Amupitan said that "algorithms will not and must never decide the 2027 vote", and that automated result checks remain subject to human auditing. The Rio Times noted that INEC has not published the unit's budget, head, staff numbers, tools or vendor.</p>

<p>Civil society groups want more detail. At a virtual discussion hosted by <a href="https://factcheckhub.com/experts-warn-ai-driven-disinformation-threatens-2027-elections-call-for-safeguards/" {A}>FactCheckHub</a> the same day, Yiaga Africa executive director Samson Itodo said INEC had not adequately explained its AI adoption strategy and that its approach "should be transparent and open to public scrutiny". He warned that AI-made content could be used to delegitimise elections and to discourage people from voting.</p>

<h2>The wider election picture</h2>

<p>INEC said it has procured about 57,000 new BVAS accreditation devices and reduced the number of voters per device from 1,250 to 850 to shorten queues. It plans to deploy about 1.41 million ad hoc staff. The presidential and National Assembly elections are scheduled for 16 January 2027, with governorship and state assembly polls on 6 February.</p>

<h2>What it means for Nigerian and African voters</h2>

<p>Nigeria's vote will be one of the first major African elections held with cheap, convincing AI tools widely available. How INEC, platforms and the media handle fake audio and video will be watched by electoral bodies across the continent.</p>

<p>Detection tools help, but speed and trust matter more. Fake clips spread in minutes, often on WhatsApp, where they are hard to track. The most effective defence is a commission that publishes accurate information quickly and explains how its own technology works.</p>

<p>For voters, the advice is practical: be wary of shocking audio or video released close to polling day, check INEC's official channels and established fact-checkers before sharing, and remember that results are collated and published through official processes, not viral images.</p>
"""),
