dict(
slot="Nigeria News", label="NIGERIA NEWS", cats=[163],
slug="tinubu-pledges-rescue-abducted-borno-schoolchildren-mussa-lassa",
title="Tinubu Pledges to Rescue Schoolchildren Abducted in Borno's Mussa and Lassa as Families Wait for News",
excerpt="Speaking in Maiduguri, President Bola Tinubu said the government would do everything possible to free the pupils taken from two schools in Askira-Uba, who have been held for months.",
kw="Borno schoolchildren abducted, Mussa Lassa abduction, Askira-Uba schools, Tinubu rescue pledge, school safety Nigeria",
seo_title="Tinubu Pledges Rescue of Abducted Borno Schoolchildren",
desc="President Tinubu has pledged to rescue schoolchildren abducted from Mussa and Lassa in Borno's Askira-Uba. What is known about the children, held for months.",
tags=["Nigeria","Nigeria News","Borno","Askira-Uba","School Abduction","Mussa","Lassa","Bola Tinubu","Insecurity","North-East Nigeria","Education","Schoolchildren"],
html=f"""
<p><strong>MAIDUGURI</strong> — President Bola Tinubu has pledged that his government will do everything possible to rescue schoolchildren abducted from two communities in Borno State's Askira-Uba Local Government Area, who have been held for months.</p>

<p>"We shall do everything possible to rescue the schoolchildren abducted in Mussa and Lassa communities," Tinubu said on Wednesday, 25 November, <a href="https://dailypost.ng/2026/11/25/well-do-everything-possible-to-rescue-abducted-borno-pupils-tinubu/" {A}>Daily Post reported</a>. He spoke at the All Progressives Congress (APC) North-East zonal campaign rally at the El-Kanemi Sports Centre in Maiduguri.</p>

<h2>How many children, and since when</h2>

<p>Reports differ on the numbers and dates. <a href="https://saharareporters.com/2026/11/25/tinubu-apc-campaign-vows-rescue-78-borno-schoolchildren-abducted-may" {A}>Sahara Reporters</a> put the total at 78: about 42 children taken from Mussa on 15 May 2026 and 36 from Lassa on 29 June 2026, meaning the first group had spent 194 days in captivity by Wednesday. Daily Post described "about 70" children, including toddlers from Mussa and students from Lassa. <a href="https://www.thepointng.com/tinubu-vows-to-rescue-abducted-borno-schoolchildren-at-maiduguri-rally/" {A}>ThePoint</a> also reported about 70.</p>

<p>Fintech Telex has not been able to independently confirm the exact number. Federal and state authorities have not published an official list.</p>

<h2>Families waiting</h2>

<p>For the families, months have passed without public word on the children's whereabouts. Neither the federal government nor the Borno State government has given a public timeline for their release.</p>

<p>Tinubu also praised troops on the frontline and promised continued support. "The present administration under my leadership acknowledges the sacrifices of troops, especially those at the frontline. Therefore, government will do its best to support our security operatives to defeat terrorism in this region," he said, according to Daily Post.</p>

<p>Borno has been at the centre of Nigeria's insurgency for more than a decade, and school attacks in the North-East have repeatedly forced classrooms to close.</p>

<h2>A long pattern of school attacks</h2>

<p>Mass school abductions have marked Nigeria's security crisis for more than a decade, from Chibok in 2014 to Dapchi, Kankara and Kuriga. Some children were freed within weeks after negotiations or military operations; others were held for years. Each case has renewed calls for better protection of schools in conflict areas and for clearer communication with families while rescue efforts are under way.</p>

<p>Askira-Uba, in southern Borno, sits close to areas where insurgent groups remain active, and communities there have faced repeated attacks. Rescue operations in such terrain carry risks for both the children and the troops involved, which is one reason authorities often say little in public while efforts continue.</p>

<h2>What it means for Nigeria</h2>

<p>School abductions do lasting damage beyond the families directly affected. Parents in insecure areas keep children at home, schools close, and communities lose a generation of learning. That deepens poverty in regions that already have some of Nigeria's lowest school enrolment rates.</p>

<p>The president's pledge came at a campaign rally, seven weeks before national elections, which means security in the North-East is likely to remain a central campaign issue. Opposition parties and civil society groups are likely to judge the government on results rather than promises.</p>

<p>The key questions now are whether authorities will share more information with families, and whether there is a clear, accountable plan to bring the children home safely. Fintech Telex will report any confirmed developments.</p>
"""),
